# ssh/aliases.tmpl — the alias-contract PLANE. Rendered by scripts/harness/seed-seat-ssh-config.sh into
# ONE marker-delimited block inside a seat's ~/.ssh/config. It is not an ssh config and is never
# installed as one.
#
# WHY A PLANE AND NOT A README. The prose SSOT for these blocks is
# [../docs/network-vpn.md](../docs/network-vpn.md) §The laptop alias contract, and for a year the
# blocks themselves lived only in three hand-kept seat files, which is how they drifted: a
# duplicate `Host ap-dnevna`, a missing `ap-spare`, a `Host pi99` still logging in as the
# neutralized Pi `admin`, and a seat with no self-alias at all. Prose cannot fail, so none of that
# was ever reported. This file is the same contract in the one form a script can diff.
#
# TEMPLATE GRAMMAR — the whole language, three rules (the renderer enforces it and fails loud on
# an unknown placeholder or an unterminated region):
#   NAME at-signs  a placeholder (the name wrapped in at-signs) replaced from the seat-class
#                  table in scripts/harness/seed-seat-ssh-config.sh — an unresolved one fails the render
#   #% seat: a b   emit the following lines only for classes a/b; a following `#% seat:` line
#                  starts the next region; `#% end` returns to "every class"
#   anything else  emitted verbatim for every class
#
# ADDRESSES are literals HERE on purpose: an ssh payload reads no group_vars, and the SSOT rows
# they come from are [../docs/network-addresses-generated.md](../docs/network-addresses-generated.md).
# What this file buys is that the address is repeated in ONE repo file instead of three seat files.
#
# IDENTITY FILE NAMES are not free text: every `IdentityFile` here is a canonical name from
# [../docs/network-vpn.md](../docs/network-vpn.md) §Canonical identity file names, and the
# renderer refuses a render that names any other file or any path that is not `~/.ssh/…`.

# Four rules decide every block (measurements live in the doc section above):
#   1. A NAME decides the hop; an ADDRESS is always direct. The plain node name reaches its HOME
#      (VLAN 10) leg with NO jump — on site the seat and every node share the Home /24, the laptop's
#      Home leg being a static `dhcp-10` reservation. Address VALUES belong to the rendered address map,
#      and a live reservation no ledger row names is a repo defect, not a value to copy into a template
#      — and the `<node>-vps` twin adds `ProxyJump vps` for the away case. The Mgmt leg is never an
#      away path (HD-398 decision A), and a `10.10.1.x` address away is a dead ADDRESS, not a dead
#      host: off-site you use the name (or the `-vps` name), never the address.
#   2. OpenSSH matches the name ACTUALLY TYPED, so a node gets an alias block AND a block for the
#      address a human, `scp` or `rsync` would type (Ansible needs neither: its jump travels in
#      `ansible_ssh_common_args`, which is exactly why flipping these seat names changes no
#      converge — HD-397);
#   3. the Mgmt plane (VLAN 99) is a same-site plane: its aliases are jump-less, and a `ProxyJump`
#      on a `10.10.99.x` alias converts a fast failure into a ~90 s banner timeout. Never add one;
#   4. the `-vps` twins are emitted on the LAPTOP classes only. A host seat lives in the house, and
#      a jumped path from oldsrv to a node on its own subnet is a hairpin through the internet that
#      nothing has ever measured.

# --- the jump -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The only public address in the contract. `AddressFamily inet` is not decoration (HD-407):
# `vps.kogler.si` carries an AAAA, a dual-stack seat prefers it, TCP/22 to it times out, and every
# jumped leg — including the seat's own address — then reads `Connection timed out during banner
# exchange`. The laptop never hits it because it has no IPv6 at all; the line is there so a seat
# that grows an IPv6 route does not discover it as a mystery.
Host vps
  HostName vps.kogler.si
  User ansible-admin
  IdentityFile @ADMIN@
  IdentitiesOnly yes
  AddressFamily inet

# --- pi: the human leg on a laptop, the admin leg on a host seat ----------------
# `User domen` since HD-443 neutralized the Pi's `admin` account: a `Host pi99`/`Host pi` still
# naming `admin` is the bug this plane carries as a quick fix, because the account is disabled on
# the box and the alias is the only thing that never noticed.
Host pi @PI_PAT@
  HostName @PI_HN@
  User @PI_USER@
  IdentityFile @PI_ID@
  IdentitiesOnly yes
#% seat: cockpit
  AddressFamily inet
#% end
#% seat: laptop-win11 laptop-wsl
Host pi-vps
  HostName @PI_HN@
  User @PI_USER@
  IdentityFile @PI_ID@
  IdentitiesOnly yes
  AddressFamily inet
  ProxyJump vps
#% end

